{"timestamp":"2026-04-25T13:23:48.509Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yes, but I would not use them as the primary in-process redactor.\n\nBest alternatives:\n\n1. **TruffleHog**\n   It has DB-oriented detectors for MongoDB, Postgres, Redis, JDBC, SQL Server, plus a URI detector for HTTP(S) URLs with embedded credentials. That is much closer to the database gap than gitleaks/Betterleaks defaults.\n   Caveat: TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0, heavy as an embedded dependency, and some detectors are verification-oriented. I would not casually vendor it into the CLI without a license/product review.\n   Source: https://github.com/trufflesecurity/trufflehog\n\n2. **GitGuardian / ggshield**\n   GitGuardian has explicit detectors for “Generic Database Assignment,” MongoDB, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Redis, MSSQL, DB2, Snowflake, Supabase, etc. This is probably the strongest off-the-shelf DB-secret coverage.\n   Caveat: it is more SaaS/API/CLI oriented, not a clean local Go redaction library for transcript processing.\n   Source: https://docs.gitguardian.com/secrets-detection/secrets-detection-engine/detectors/supported_credentials\n\n3. **First-party DB redaction rules**\n   This is my recommendation for Entire. Implement deterministic local redaction for:\n   - `REDACTED`\n   - `jdbc:*`\n   - `REDACTED   - `REDACTED   - `DB_PASSWORD=REDACTED, `PGPASSWORD=REDACTED, `REDIS_PASSWORD=REDACTED   - query params like `?password=...`\n\nReason: transcript redaction needs to be offline, fast, deterministic, and conservative. External scanners are useful as an additional layer, but DB credentials are common enough and structured enough that Entire should own those rules directly."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
