{"timestamp":"2026-04-25T13:39:59.193Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Overredaction risk is real, especially for generic `password=...` rules. The right approach is not “redact every word after password anywhere”; it’s structured, bounded matching.\n\nLowest-risk rules:\n- `REDACTED`: low false-positive risk because a password-bearing URI is almost always sensitive.\n- `jdbc:*` with `password=` or `user:pass@host`: low to medium risk.\n- SQL Server/ODBC strings with multiple connection-string keys: low risk if requiring `Server/Data Source` plus `User/User Id/UID` plus `Password/PWD`.\n- Postgres keyword DSN requiring at least `host`, `user`, and `password`: low risk.\n\nHigher-risk rules:\n- Any standalone `password=...`\n- `secret=...`\n- `token=...`\n- `key=...`\n\nFor those, I’d constrain them:\n- Only redact values for credential-like keys, not whole lines.\n- Stop at clear delimiters: whitespace, newline, quote, comma, semicolon, `&`.\n- Require non-empty values and ignore placeholders like `xxx`, `REDACTED`, `changeme`, `${DB_PASSWORD}`.\n- For generic keys, prefer redacting only when context suggests config/env/command output, not prose.\n- Keep path/ID skip rules intact so structural transcript fields don’t get corrupted.\n\nRecommended policy for Entire: bias toward redaction for transcript content, but avoid destructive whole-line redaction except for full connection strings. For generic key/value secrets, redact just the value:\n\n```text\nDB_PASSWORD=secret\nDB_PASSWORD=REDACTED\n```\n\nnot:\n\n```text\nREDACTED\n```\n\nThat preserves context while removing the sensitive part."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}

